var t=[{title:`Three categories`,text:`Anti-extension work stops your lower back from arching too much, as in a plank or a dead bug. Anti-rotation work resists twisting, as in a Pallof press with a safely anchored band. Anti-lateral-flexion work resists bending sideways, as in a side plank or a suitcase carry. The abdominal wall, spinal muscles, diaphragm and hips all help control the trunk.`},{title:`Technique examples`,text:`In a forearm plank, line up your ribs and pelvis, press the floor away through your arms, and keep breathing while you hold steady. In a dead bug, move the opposite arm and leg only as far as your trunk stays controlled. In a side plank, hold a straight, supported line and don’t let your hips drop.`},{title:`Progressions`,text:`Start with short holds and short levers. A knee-supported side plank can come before a full side plank, and a dead bug with bent knees before straight-leg reaches. Make it harder by lengthening the lever, adding a moderate load or holding longer under control. If your posture breaks, the exercise has gotten too hard. Watch your breathing and alignment as you progress.`},{title:`Beyond planks`,text:`Crunches and other trunk-flexion exercises also train the abdominals, but they don’t cover every job of the core. Rows, squats and carries also make your trunk work to stay stable. You don’t need to train every direction in one session. Include a few controlled core exercises across the week, chosen for your experience and how your lower back tolerates them. Doing them consistently and progressing at a pace you tolerate matters more.`}];var r=[{label:`ACE exercise library`,url:`https://www.acefitness.org/resources/everyone/exercise-library/`},{label:`NSCA: safety and resistance-training fundamentals`,url:`https://www.nsca.com/education/articles/ptq/the-safest-and-riskiest-forms-of-resistance-training/`},{label:`2026 ACSM position stand`,url:`https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/41843416/`}];var s={sections:t,sources:r};export{s as default,t as sections,r as sources};